**Question:** Should surrounding whitespace in CSV cells be trimmed before validation, identity matching, and duplicate comparison, or treated as part of the value?

Why it matters: it decides whether inputs like `" USD "`, `" Acme Cleaning Co"`, or `" INV-1001 "` are valid and whether two otherwise-equal rows count as identical duplicates.

**Recommended:** Option A - trimming tolerates common export padding while keeping case and internal characters significant, so identity matching stays deterministic.

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| A | Trim leading/trailing whitespace on all seven fields before validation and duplicate comparison; case and internal characters remain significant. |
| B | Do not trim; raw cell text is the value for validation and comparison, so surrounding whitespace makes a value invalid or distinct. |
| C | Trim only text fields (issuer, invoice_id, dates, currency); numeric fields must match exactly. |
| Short | Provide a different short answer (<=5 words). |

You can reply with the option letter (e.g., "A"), accept the recommendation by saying "yes" or "recommended", or provide your own short answer.

